    "result_text": "All 3 tests pass with a green build.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented address normalization for owner creation by following the codebase's existing convention for input-field normalization (the `TelephoneNormalizer` \u2192 `OwnerMapper` `@Named` qualifier pattern).\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **New `AddressNormalizer`** (`rest/validation/AddressNormalizer.java`) \u2014 a small, single-responsibility utility (mirroring `TelephoneNormalizer`) that trims, collapses internal whitespace, upper-cases, and expands whole-word abbreviations (`ST\u2192STREET`, `RD\u2192ROAD`, `AVE\u2192AVENUE`).\n\n2. **`OwnerMapper`** \u2014 wired the normalizer into `toOwner(OwnerFieldsDto)` via a `@Named(\"normalizeAddress\")` default method and an address `@Mapping`, exactly parallel to how telephone is normalized. This is the single point where owner-creation input becomes the stored `Owner`.\n\n**How each requirement is satisfied:**\n\n- **Store/return normalized address** \u2014 the mapper normalizes at creation, so the persisted and returned `address` is normalized (verified by `\"  12  main  st \"` \u2192 `\"12 MAIN STREET\"` and `\"7 elm ave\"` \u2192 `\"7 ELM AVENUE\"`).\n- **Required-field check on normalized-blank** \u2014 the generated `@Valid` on the request body enforces the DTO's `@NotBlank` on `address`, rejecting whitespace-only input (`\"   \"` \u2192 400). Since normalization only trims/collapses/upper-cases/expands, no non-blank input can normalize to blank, so this is equivalent to checking the normalized form.\n- **Comparisons use the normalized form** \u2014 both household duplicate detection (`existsOwnerInHousehold`/`findHouseholdMembers`) and the shared `householdId` derivation read `owner.getAddress()`, which is now normalized before the owner reaches the service layer. No service changes were needed since they already consumed the owner's address.\n\nI deliberately kept the logic out of `ClinicServiceImpl` and the controllers (which would otherwise become catch-alls) and reused the established normalizer/mapper pattern rather than duplicating string handling.",
